course: dictionary
lesson: 9359
title: Spoilers and their meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A spoiler is information that tells you what happens in a story, film or game before you have seen it. «A spoiler tells the ending before someone has seen it; it is annoying, and spoiling on purpose is a small meanness that needs a "sorry".» The clue is the ending: a spoiler gives it away too soon.
explain: «A spoiler is when someone tells you the ending before you get there.» Read the words around spoiler to check: if the sentence is about a film, book or game and someone reveals what happens, the meaning is the spoiler from the story.
explain: Spoiler has other meanings too. On a car or plane, a spoiler is a flat part that changes the air flow to keep it steady or slow it down. Some people use it for a person who spoils things for others. Use the context to decide which meaning fits.
fact: A spoiler is information that tells what happens in a story, film or game before you have seen it.
fact: People write «spoiler alert!» before giving away an ending, so others can stop reading.
fact: «No spoilers» and «spoiler-free review» mean the writer does not tell the ending.
fact: A rear spoiler is a flat part on the back of a race car that pushes the car down onto the road.
fact: A spoiler is also someone who spoils things for others.
exercise: Sam writes «spoiler alert!» before he tells the ending of the film. Why does he write it? | To show he has a new phone | To tell everyone how much he liked the film | To ask for a snack | *To warn others so they can stop reading before the ending
exercise: The race car has a big spoiler on its back. What does the spoiler do? | *It pushes the car down onto the road to keep it steady | It keeps the rain off the driver | It changes the colour of the car | It makes the car float like a boat
exercise: Tom tells the whole class the ending of the film on purpose. What is Tom? | A director | *A spoiler | A critic | A cinema
test: In politics, what is a spoiler? | *A candidate who cannot win but takes votes away from another candidate | A teacher who marks the papers | A person who writes the news on television | A candidate who wins every vote
test: Which is the best way to write a review without giving the ending away? | Give away the best twist | *Say nothing about the ending | Tell the ending first | Write the ending in capital letters
exam: Which word fits the gap? «Please don't ___ the ending, I haven't seen the film yet!» | spoiled | *spoil | spoiler | spoilt
